{"reference":"Haggai 1","book":"Haggai","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Haggai confronts a community that says it is not yet time to rebuild YHWH’s house. The chapter contrasts that delay with attention to private homes, then calls the people to reconsider their course, gather timber, and build. Its account moves from rebuke and scarcity to obedience: Zerubbabel, Joshua, and the remnant begin the work after the assurance, “I am with you.” The language about covered houses challenges the community’s priorities, though it does not prove that every person addressed lived luxuriously. The descriptions of poor harvests, inadequate food and clothing, and ineffective wages are presented in prophetic rhetoric as connected with neglect of the house.","verse_refs":["Haggai 1:1","Haggai 1:12-15","Haggai 1:3-4","Haggai 1:5-11","Haggai 1:8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_haggai_001_f3f837ccb403fc1f7cd775b9"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Historical Setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The opening date places this word in the second year of Darius I. Persian royal inscriptions and the Cyrus Cylinder illuminate how Achaemenid kingship could be associated with imperial rule, divine patronage, and restoration of cultic life. They provide a broader setting for the royal date in Haggai, but they do not directly document the temple work described here.","verse_refs":["Haggai 1:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_haggai_001_f76479a97a2e1ea0d56cacdc"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"},{"id":"block_3","text":"Evidence from archaeology and administration portrays early Persian-period Yehud as materially smaller and economically vulnerable. That setting can help readers hear why scarcity and labor are so prominent in the chapter, while also leaving important details—such as population, taxation, drought, and labor conditions—as reconstructions rather than information fully narrated by Haggai.","verse_refs":["Haggai 1:1","Haggai 1:2-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_haggai_001_e2955123d2a502d8d4311965"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Literary Features","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"“Consider your ways” is a repeated command to set one’s heart on one’s course. It asks the audience to interpret the relation the speech draws among labor, scarcity, worship, and rebuilding. The book also alternates quoted prophetic speech with prose framing; scholars differ over how Haggai’s oracles, narrative reports, later editing, and the book’s shaping within the Twelve relate to one another. The text calls Haggai a prophet and YHWH’s messenger, but gives no further biographical detail such as ancestry, birthplace, age, or prior office.","verse_refs":["Haggai 1:1","Haggai 1:13","Haggai 1:2","Haggai 1:5-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_haggai_001_9b6bf43568c96a0150803777"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}